<LoreDoc title="How to Contribute">
  <p>
    Hashiverse has no company behind it and no servers of its own. Its resilience, availability,
    and censorship resistance are entirely a function of how many independent servers exist in the
    network. Every node you add makes the network harder to attack, harder to silence, and more
    useful for everyone on it. If you believe in what Hashiverse is trying to be, running a server
    is the most direct way to help.
  </p>
  <p>
    The good news: it costs about as much as a cup of coffee a month. And if you want to
    contribute more, the right move is to run <em>more</em> servers, not a bigger one —
    ten $5 machines spread across different providers and regions are far more valuable to
    the network than one $50 machine.
  </p>

  <h2>What you need</h2>
  <p>
    Any cheap VPS from any provider will do — Hetzner, DigitalOcean, Vultr, Linode, and many
    others offer suitable machines for around $5/month. The minimum requirements are modest:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Storage:</strong> 20 GB of disk space</li>
    <li><strong>Bandwidth:</strong> 1 TB per month</li>
    <li><strong>OS:</strong> Ubuntu/Debian or Fedora/RHEL (or any modern Linux with Docker)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    CPU and RAM requirements are minimal — the server is written in Rust and is designed to run
    efficiently on the smallest available instance sizes.
  </p>

    <h2>Installing and running the server</h2>
    <p>
        An important point to note is that when you fire up a server, it has to do some significant
        Proof of Work before it is designated a trusted spot in the Hashiverse network.  So don't be
        surprised if for the first few hours your CPU is at max and the logs keep talking about PoWs, iters, and ETA.
    </p>
    <p>
        We recommend any standard linux server.  Ubuntu works nicely, either 2024, or 2026.
        Paste the following into the startup script / user-data / cloud-init field when you
        create your server (the name varies by provider). It opens the firewall, adds the signed
        Hashiverse package repository, and installs the server, which starts straight away.
    </p>
    <pre><code>#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
apt-get update
apt-get install -y curl ca-certificates ufw

ufw allow 22/tcp
ufw allow from 0.0.0.0/0 to any port 443 proto tcp
ufw --force enable

curl -fsSL https://www.hashiverse.com/packages/hashiverse-packages.asc -o /usr/share/keyrings/hashiverse-packages.asc
curl -fsSL https://www.hashiverse.com/packages/apt/hashiverse.sources -o /etc/apt/sources.list.d/hashiverse.sources
curl -fsSL https://www.hashiverse.com/packages/apt/hashiverse-mirrors.txt -o /etc/apt/hashiverse-mirrors.txt

apt-get update
apt-get install -y hashiverse-server</code></pre>
  <p>
    On Fedora, Rocky, Alma or RHEL, add the repository and install with <code>dnf</code> instead
    (and open port 443 in <code>firewalld</code>):
  </p>
  <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://www.hashiverse.com/packages/hashiverse-packages.asc -o /etc/pki/rpm-gpg/RPM-GPG-KEY-hashiverse
curl -fsSL https://www.hashiverse.com/packages/rpm/hashiverse.repo -o /etc/yum.repos.d/hashiverse.repo
dnf install -y hashiverse-server</code></pre>
  <p>
    The server runs as the systemd service <code>hashiverse-server</code> and keeps itself up to
    date: once a day, at a random time that differs from machine to machine so the network never
    restarts all at once, it checks for a new version and restarts onto it. A few useful commands:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Follow the logs:</strong> <code>journalctl -u hashiverse-server -f</code></li>
    <li><strong>Settings:</strong> <code>/etc/default/hashiverse-server</code>, then <code>systemctl restart hashiverse-server</code></li>
    <li><strong>Data and server identity:</strong> <code>/var/lib/hashiverse-server</code></li>
    <li><strong>Turn off automatic updates:</strong> <code>systemctl disable --now hashiverse-server-updater.timer</code></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The server's built-in DDoS protection uses <code>ipset</code> and <code>iptables</code> to drop
    traffic from abusive IPs at the kernel level, so it runs as root, but in a systemd sandbox that
    only grants it the networking capabilities it needs. <code>/var/lib/hashiverse-server</code> is
    where the server persists its identity and stored encrypted posts — keep it safe if you care about
    continuity, although Hashiverse is designed to heal itself if you destroy a server and fire up a new one.
  </p>

  <h3>Alternatively, with Docker</h3>
  <p>
    If you'd rather run the server in Docker, this startup script installs Docker, opens the
    firewall, writes the compose file, and brings the stack up.
  </p>
  <pre><code>#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
apt-get update
apt-get install -y docker.io docker-compose-v2 ufw

ufw allow 22/tcp
ufw allow from 0.0.0.0/0 to any port 443 proto tcp
ufw --force enable

mkdir -p /opt/hashiverse && cd /opt/hashiverse

cat &gt; docker-compose.yml &lt;&lt;"YAML"
services:
  hashiverse-server:
    image: ghcr.io/hashiverse/hashiverse-server:latest
    restart: unless-stopped
    stop_grace_period: 30s
    ports:
      - "0.0.0.0:443:443"
    cap_add:
      - NET_ADMIN
    volumes:
      - ./data:/data
    logging:
      driver: local
      options:
        max-size: 10m
        max-file: "5"

  watchtower:
    image: nickfedor/watchtower
    restart: unless-stopped
    volumes:
      - /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock
    command: --interval 3600
YAML

docker compose pull
docker compose up -d</code></pre>
  <p>
    Watchtower will keep the server automatically updated as new versions are released.
    The <code>NET_ADMIN</code> capability is required for the server's built-in DDoS protection,
    and the <code>./data</code> volume is where the server persists its identity and stored
    encrypted posts.
  </p>
</LoreDoc>
